{"type":"message_update","assistantMessageEvent":{"type":"thinking_end","contentIndex":0,"content":"Let me explain in two sentences why the original order led to excessive grants (over-granting).\n\nThe original order was as follows: release → pop the waiter from _wait_queue → event.set(). The woken waiter only adds itself to _borrowers after `await event.wait()` resumes. Since the event loop doesn't yield between `event.set()` and the resumption, a synchronous nowait acquire that executes during that interval sees an empty `_wait_queue` and `len(_borrowers) < total` (the woken borrower is not yet registered), so its guard passes and ends up adding a second borrower for the same single token.\n\nIn other words, the reservation of the token was delayed until the woken task resumed, but the token was already logically consumed — the guard's two signals (queue membership and borrower set) were both false during the handoff interval, and the token temporarily appeared to be free.\n\nTwo sentences, concisely.\n"}}
